The Law of Large Numbers states that, for any unlikely event X, the
probability of X occurring at least once increases as the number of
events increases. For this reason, miracles happen every month, people
do win the lottery, and lightning strikes twice in the same place.

The Gambler's Fallacy is a belif in the "memory" of probability. After
Red comes up at roulette, 3 dozen times in a row, red should be
slightly less likely to come up, says the fallacy. In all fact, the
probability is still unchanged: 9/19 for red, 9/19 for black, 1/19 for
0/00.



